[#:seon.task{:name "core-contracts-with-an-undefined-slot"
             :plan "`seon.cluster.source/uncontracted` now judges a merge's changed functions by `seon.fn/contract-findings`, so an unjustified `:any` or `:some` slot (`:seon.fn.contract.finding/any`, `:seon.fn.contract.finding/some`) refuses the merge. On default at 4a1fc2e7e (2026-10-13), 53 functions in 26 namespaces still hold one, seon.fs/intermediate-paths among them until its repair in the same commit (count: `seon.fn/contract-findings` over every function, those two findings); an agent that changes one of them must complete its contract before its merge is accepted. Done when `seon.contracts/gaps` answers no `:seon.fn.contract.finding/any` or `:seon.fn.contract.finding/some` row for any of them: each slot named by a registered schema or named predicate, or a real polymorphic boundary justified by `:seon.schema.admission/exemption`, its reason and a bounded generator. Generate one task per function with `seon.contracts/task`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "generated-task-text-names-the-plan"
             :plan "After the S1a rename (243683f1d) of `:seon.task/intent` to `:seon.task/plan`, the text `seon.contracts/task` generates for a function still says \"in its intent\" (the private `seon.contracts/plan`, \"file a task on the producer naming `...` in its intent\"), and every committed `contract-*` task (12 task files hold one) carries the old wording. Change the generator's wording to \"in its plan\" at its producer, then regenerate every committed `contract-*` entry through `seon.contracts/task` so the files equal what the generator now answers; do not hand-edit those files. Done when no task file under `resources/seon/tasks` holds `in its intent` and `seon.contracts/task` of a sampled function equals its committed entry. Proof: to write before assignment (a deftest comparing each committed `contract-*` entry's `:seon.task/plan` with the generator's answer). Read `seon.contracts/task` and `seon.task/effective`."}
